                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1128c0fe-b357-3c72-8c04-b5b0730bc99a",
                        "entry_id": "7881192a-0b60-30e1-ae12-a266cca35a72",
                        "glosses": [
                            "przeciwnik kultu obrazów, wizerunków i figur religijnych"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1.1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5d8b838a-7769-493f-b8ca-0fc0d3f83aac",
                        "source_entry_id": "7881192a-0b60-30e1-ae12-a266cca35a72",
                        "source_sense_id": "1128c0fe-b357-3c72-8c04-b5b0730bc99a",
                        "target_lang_word": "iconoclast",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "iconoclast",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5f5c6d10-4d9d-3c60-8260-2c0c8702e5cf",
                                        "entry_id": "e3b990a7-d4d6-341f-8c03-28f858f30798",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One who destroys religious images or icons, especially an opponent of the Orthodox Church in the 8th and 9th centuries, or a Puritan during the European Reformation."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0d683e8a-3105-4275-a34f-93741e9d1580",
                                                "sense_id": "5f5c6d10-4d9d-3c60-8260-2c0c8702e5cf",
                                                "text": "In the days of the early Christian church, people who opposed the veneration (reverence) of images were called iconoclasts."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "16d54642-163b-36ba-8d23-e4061ecf7dbb",
                                        "entry_id": "e3b990a7-d4d6-341f-8c03-28f858f30798",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One who opposes orthodoxy and religion; one who adheres to the doctrine of iconoclasm."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d260e6dc-5bb3-4452-8734-391596c73745",
                                                "sense_id": "16d54642-163b-36ba-8d23-e4061ecf7dbb",
                                                "text": "In February 1895 he [William Cowper Brann, 1855-1898 ] revived publication of the Iconoclast. This time it was successful and eventually attained a circulation of 100,000. Brann took obvious relish in directing his stinging attacks upon institutions and persons he considered to be hypocritical or overly sanctimonious."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "703cf888-679d-3199-9bcd-a61da6a8713a",
                                        "entry_id": "e3b990a7-d4d6-341f-8c03-28f858f30798",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One who attacks cherished beliefs; a maverick."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b0c227e4-729c-48d0-882b-d4422be0d833",
                                                "sense_id": "703cf888-679d-3199-9bcd-a61da6a8713a",
                                                "text": "After all, the fact that any discussion of the possibility is greeted with hysterical revulsion guarantees that only two types of people will take the \"pro\" side in public: fearless iconoclasts who do not care what anyone thinks of them; and racists."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9f91f29c-2d35-45bc-b356-72279547a1d9",
                                                "sense_id": "703cf888-679d-3199-9bcd-a61da6a8713a",
                                                "text": "Coming so soon after the death of David Bowie, it’s a moment to consider how deeply important iconoclasts are to the evolution of a culture. Do they change the world by trying to change it, or do they change the world through the radical act of simply being themselves?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "972a5dec-6ed0-480b-8fab-804932a50e1a",
                                                "sense_id": "703cf888-679d-3199-9bcd-a61da6a8713a",
                                                "text": "Relishing the role of iconoclast, he confounded the scientific establishment by dismissing the consensus about the perils of man-made climate change as “tribal group-thinking.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
